<Routines>
<Routine Name="EnableInFalse" Type="RLL">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Disabled is asserted, Output OFF (zero), Alarms disabled]]>
</Description>
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
OFF-SCAN (EnableIn is False) STATUS
============================================================
If this device is OFF-SCAN (rung is false or EnableIn is false in FBD), then:

Handle "NO MODE" Selection using the standard P_Mode AOI (EnableInFalse Routine, false scan required!).

NOTE:  IN ORDER TO AVOID USING AN "AFI" (which will generate a routine verify warning),
THIS RUNG USES AN XIC of Cfg_PCmdClear, followed by an XIO of the SAME BIT
to ensure P_Mode is SCANNED FALSE ("NO MODE").

THIS IS NOT DEAD CODE AND MUST APPEAR EXACTLY AS SHOWN FOR PROPER OPERATION!]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Cfg_PCmdClear)XIO(Cfg_PCmdClear)P_Mode(Mode);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[First-Come First-Served Ownership Arbitration still needs to happen, even when device is disabled.
If a requestor supplies a non-zero Owner ID and the current owner is NONE (zero),
Ownership is assigned to the requesting ID.  When that requestor sets the Owner ID back
to zero, Ownership is relinquished back to NONE.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[EQU(PSet_Owner,0) ,EQU(Val_Owner,0) ]MOV(PSet_Owner,Val_Owner);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
CONFIGURATION
============================================================
These rungs get Configuration data (such as timer presets) and distribute as needed.
Note that some Configuration data are used directly in-place.

This rung publishes the Values for the scaled range.
If this instruction is reverse scaled by reversing the scaled (EU) min and max configurations,
the values are swapped so the the EUMax Value is always greater than the EUMin Value.
This makes the HMI bargraph and trend coding easier, and provides values
that can be pinned to the CV EU configuration of a PID, so the AOut and PID ranges are in lockstep.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[GEQ(Cfg_CVEUMax,Cfg_CVEUMin) MOV(Cfg_CVEUMin,Val_CVEUMin) MOV(Cfg_CVEUMax,Val_CVEUMax) ,LES(Cfg_CVEUMax,Cfg_CVEUMin) MOV(Cfg_CVEUMin,Val_CVEUMax) MOV(Cfg_CVEUMax,Val_CVEUMin) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Enter the maximum allowed Rate of Change Setting (in EU/sec)

If the rate is negative, infinite or not a number, no valid Rate of Change can be entered.  Set the maximum to zero.
This will force the CV Rate of Change to zero, which does NO ramping/limiting.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[LES(Cfg_MaxCVRoCInc,0.0) ,GRT(Cfg_MaxCVRoCInc,3.40282347E38) ]MOV(0.0,Cfg_MaxCVRoCInc);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Always limit the entered Rate of Change Limit to be between 0.0 and the configured Maximum.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[GRT(Wrk_CVRoCLimInc,Cfg_MaxCVRoCInc) MOV(Cfg_MaxCVRoCInc,Wrk_CVRoCLimInc) ,LES(Wrk_CVRoCLimInc,0.0) CLR(Wrk_CVRoCLimInc) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Flag a Configuration Error if the CV scaled range, or the CV output or
position feedback input range maximum and minimum are set the same (invalid range).]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[EQU(Cfg_CVEUMax,Cfg_CVEUMin) OTE(Err_EU) ,EQU(Cfg_FdbkRawMax,Cfg_FdbkRawMin) OTE(Err_Raw) ,EQU(Cfg_CVRawMax,Cfg_CVRawMin) OTE(Err_Fdbk) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Check for a valid Cycle Time.  If it results in a negative value, clear it and flag it.

The other times must be set relative to the Cycle Time.  If any is invalid, just make it valid:
* If Max ON Time < 0 or > Cycle Time, set it to Cycle Time.
*  If Min ON Time < 0 or > Max ON Time, set it to zero.
* If Deadtime < 0 or > Cycle Time, set it to zero.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[MUL(Cfg_CycleT,1000,Wrk_CycleT_ms) LES(Wrk_CycleT_ms,0) CLR(Wrk_CycleT_ms) OTE(Err_Timer) ,[LES(Cfg_MaxOnT,0.0) ,GRT(Cfg_MaxOnT,Cfg_CycleT) ] MOV(Cfg_CycleT,Cfg_MaxOnT) ,[LES(Cfg_MinOnT,0.0) ,GRT(Cfg_MinOnT,Cfg_CycleT) ] CLR(Cfg_MinOnT) ,[LES(Cfg_Deadtime,0.0) ,GRT(Cfg_Deadtime,Cfg_MaxOnT) ] CLR(Cfg_Deadtime) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="7" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Clamp other configuration parameters to ensure they are valid.

* If the Open Rate < 0, set it to zero.
* If the Close Rate < 0, set it to zero.
* Clamp the Closed/Opened status threshold to be within the
scaled range CVEUMin to CVEUMax.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[LES(Cfg_OpenRate,0.0) CLR(Cfg_OpenRate) ,LES(Cfg_CloseRate,0.0) CLR(Cfg_CloseRate) ,LES(Cfg_MaxClosedPos,Cfg_CVEUMin) MOV(Cfg_CVEUMin,Cfg_MaxClosedPos) ,GRT(Cfg_MaxClosedPos,Cfg_CVEUMax) MOV(Cfg_CVEUMax,Cfg_MaxClosedPos) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="8" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[This rung handles the reports of all Bad Configuration Status.
Individual bits are provided for various status:
Crossed CV Limits or Invalid Rate of Change Limit
Bad Cycle Time configuration
Bad Raw Scaling configs (Max = Min)
Bad Alarm configs

Then a summary Bad Config status is provided, simply an OR of the individual bits.

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22: Added Not Ready reason bits (Nrdy_xxx)  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[[LES(Cfg_MaxCV,Cfg_MinCV) ,LEQ(Cfg_MaxCVRoCInc,0.0) ] OTE(Err_Limit) ,XIC(Err_Raw) ,XIC(Err_Timer) ,[XIC(IntlkTrip.Sts_Err) ,XIC(ActuatorFault.Sts_Err) ,XIC(IOFault.Sts_Err) ] OTE(Err_Alarm) ]OTE(Sts_Err)OTE(Nrdy_CfgErr);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="9" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If this Control Valve is OFF-SCAN (rung is false or EnableIn is false in FBD), then:

Clear the pulse outputs.
Clear the Alarms
Show the Valve as "Disabled", not "Available" and "Not Ready".
Set the target position to the configured ("safe") Interlock CV value.

Other data are left in Last State!

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22: Added Not Ready reason bits (Nrdy_xxx)  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[OTU(Out_Open) OTU(Out_Close) OTU(Alm_IntlkTrip) OTU(Alm_ActuatorFault) OTU(Alm_IOFault) ,OTL(Sts_Disabled) OTL(Sts_NotRdy) OTU(Sts_Available) OTL(Sts_AlmInh) OTU(Sts_MaintByp) ,OTL(Nrdy_NoMode) OTU(Nrdy_Disabled) OTU(Nrdy_Intlk) OTU(Nrdy_IOFault) ,MOV(Cfg_IntlkCV,Wrk_CVSet) MOV(Cfg_IntlkCV,Wrk_CVOut) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="10" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
FEEDBACK PROCESSING
============================================================
Always show the actual position from the Position Feedback input.
If the input is a valid REAL number, convert it from raw units to percent open.
If the input is Infinite or Not A Number, just copy that bit pattern across.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[COP(Inp_PosFdbk,Wrk_RealChkDINT,1) MEQ(Wrk_RealChkDINT,16#7F80_0000,16#7F80_0000) OTE(Sts_PosInfNaN) ,XIC(Sts_PosInfNaN) COP(Inp_PosFdbk,Val_Pos,1) ,XIO(Sts_PosInfNaN) CPT(Val_Pos,(Inp_PosFdbk-Cfg_FdbkRawMin)*(Cfg_CVEUMax-Cfg_CVEUMin)/(Cfg_FdbkRawMax-Cfg_FdbkRawMin)+Cfg_CVEUMin) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="11" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
DEVICE FEEDBACK PROCESSING
============================================================
If the Valve is NOT being simulated and it HAS and IS USING limit switch feedback,
use the state of the limit switch feedback inputs as the Valve position.

If the Valve is being simulated or has no limit switch feedback, use the Sim Timers to provide
"fake" feedback signals based on the simulation time to Open and time to Close.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIO(Inp_Sim) [XIC(Cfg_UseOpenLS) XIC(Inp_OpenLS) ,XIO(Cfg_UseOpenLS) XIC(Cfg_UseClosedLS) XIO(Inp_ClosedLS) ] ,[XIC(Inp_Sim) ,XIO(Cfg_UseOpenLS) XIO(Cfg_UseClosedLS) ] GRT(Val_Pos,Cfg_MaxClosedPos) ]OTE(Wrk_OpenFdbk);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="12" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the Valve is NOT being simulated and it HAS and IS USING limit switch feedback,
use the state of the limit switch feedback inputs as the Valve position.

If the Valve is being simulated or has no limit switch feedback, use the Sim Timers to provide
"fake" feedback signals based on the simulation time to Open and time to Close.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIO(Inp_Sim) [XIC(Cfg_UseClosedLS) XIC(Inp_ClosedLS) ,XIO(Cfg_UseClosedLS) XIC(Cfg_UseOpenLS) XIO(Inp_OpenLS) ] ,[XIC(Inp_Sim) ,XIO(Cfg_UseClosedLS) XIO(Cfg_UseOpenLS) ] LEQ(Val_Pos,Cfg_MaxClosedPos) ]OTE(Wrk_ClosedFdbk);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="13" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
VALVE FEEDBACK STATUS
============================================================
This rung generates the Valve Feedback Status bits and Enumeration Value:
Limit Switch Fail, if the Limit Switches are in the fail position (won't be seen in Simulation)
Opened, if the valve is confirmed opened
Closed, if the valve is confirmed closed
Moving, if the Limit Switches show travel]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[[XIC(Cfg_LSFail) XIC(Wrk_OpenFdbk) XIC(Wrk_ClosedFdbk) ,XIO(Cfg_LSFail) XIO(Wrk_OpenFdbk) XIO(Wrk_ClosedFdbk) ] OTE(Sts_LSFail) MOV(3,Val_Fdbk) ,XIC(Wrk_OpenFdbk) XIO(Wrk_ClosedFdbk) MOV(2,Val_Fdbk) ,XIC(Wrk_ClosedFdbk) XIO(Wrk_OpenFdbk) OTE(Sts_Active) MOV(1,Val_Fdbk) ,[XIC(Cfg_LSFail) XIO(Wrk_OpenFdbk) XIO(Wrk_ClosedFdbk) ,XIO(Cfg_LSFail) XIC(Wrk_OpenFdbk) XIC(Wrk_ClosedFdbk) ] CLR(Val_Fdbk) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="14" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[The rate limiter's scan timer must run and clear every scan, whether the Instruction
is true or not.  Doing so here avoids a bump in the Value on transition to EnableIn TRUE.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[TON(Wrk_ScanT,?,?)CLR(Wrk_ScanT.ACC);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="15" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Alarms are turned off when off-scan, so clear the Notification Level.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[CLR(Val_Notify);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="16" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA["Lurking" Operator Commands are cleared, as well as a Program Reset.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[OTU(OCmd_Bypass) OTU(OCmd_Check) OTU(MCmd_Disable) OTU(MCmd_Enable) ,OTU(OCmd_Reset) OTU(OCmd_ResetAckAll) OTU(PCmd_Reset) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="17" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Since we're not processing Operator Commands or Settings, we're not READY for any of them either!]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[OTU(Rdy_Bypass) OTU(Rdy_Check) OTU(Rdy_Disable) OTU(Rdy_Enable) ,OTU(Rdy_Reset) OTU(Rdy_ResetAckAll) OTU(Rdy_CV) OTU(Rdy_CVRoCLimInc) OTU(Rdy_CVRoCLimDec) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="18" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[This rung generates the enumerated Device Status:
0 = At Target
1 = Ramping Down
2 = Ramping Up
3 = Clamped at CVMin
4 = Clamped at CVMax
33 = Disabled (here or in EnableInFalse routine)

And this rung generates the enumerated Device Fault Status:
0 = none
32 = I/O Fault
34 = Configuration Error]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[MOV(33,Val_Sts) ,XIO(Sts_Err) CLR(Val_Fault) ,XIC(Sts_Err) MOV(34,Val_Fault) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="19" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
ENUMERATED SOURCE / QUALITY VALUES
============================================================
Source and Quality for Input / Output values / status (SrcQ_IO)

0 = Good: live, confirmed good;   1 = Good: live, assumed good;
2 = Good: no feedback, assumed good;   8 = Test: Simulated;   9 = Test: Loopback;
10 = Test: Manually Entered;   16 = Uncertain: Live, off-spec;
17 = Uncertain, Substituted at Device / Bus;   18 = Uncertain, Substituted at AOI;
19 = Uncertain, using last known good;    20 = Uncertain, using replacement value;
32 = Bad, Signal Failure;   33 = Bad, Channel Fault;
34 = Bad, Module / Comms Fault;   35 = Bad, Invalid Configuration

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22:  Added rung for Source/Quality enumerations.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[CLR(Wrk_SrcQ) ,[XIO(Cfg_HasOpenLS) ,XIO(Cfg_HasClosedLS) ] MOV(1,Wrk_SrcQ) ,XIO(Cfg_HasOpenLS) XIO(Cfg_HasClosedLS) MOV(2,Wrk_SrcQ) ,XIC(Inp_ActuatorFault) MOV(32,Wrk_SrcQ) ,XIC(Inp_IOFault) MOV(34,Wrk_SrcQ) ,XIC(Inp_Sim) MOV(9,Wrk_SrcQ) ,MOV(Wrk_SrcQ,SrcQ_IO) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="20" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Source and Quality for primary values / status (SrcQ)

0 = Good: live, confirmed good;   1 = Good: live, assumed good;
2 = Good: no feedback, assumed good;   8 = Test: Simulated;   9 = Test: Loopback;
10 = Test: Manually Entered;   16 = Uncertain: Live, off-spec;
17 = Uncertain, Substituted at Device / Bus;   18 = Uncertain, Substituted at AOI;
19 = Uncertain, using last known good;    20 = Uncertain, using replacement value;
32 = Bad, Signal Failure;   33 = Bad, Channel Fault;
34 = Bad, Module / Comms Fault;   35 = Bad, Invalid Configuration

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22:  Added rung for Source/Quality enumerations.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(20,SrcQ);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="Logic" Type="RLL">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Normal Scan: control Analog Output]]>
</Description>
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Copyright © Rockwell Automation, Inc.  All Rights Reserved.
============================================================
P_ValveC :  PROCESS -- CONTROL VALVE
============================================================
Revision 3.1-02 Release  2014-09-30:  See V3.1-xx Release Notes for details.
V2.x Revision History archived.
V3.0-00  2013-06-30:  Added Inf_Lib, Cfg_HasCVNav, Cfg_CVNavTag.
Using P_Alarm, P_Mode V3.0.
V3.0-01  2013-07-31:  Modified Parameter descriptions only.
V3.0-02  2013-09-03:  Modified Parameter descriptions only.
V3.0-03  2013-10-30: Corrected Parameter descriptions and
default configuration values for alarms only.
V3.0-04  2014-01-22:  Corrected generation of Val_Sts, Val_Fault.
Added configuration note to Instruction Help.
V3.1-00  2014-07-22:  Modified to use P_Alarm, P_Mode V3.1; added
Not Ready reason bits (Nrdy_Xxxxx), source/quality values (SrcQ).
Modified to consistently use alias tag names.  Added check for
Err_Fdbk on scaling of position feedback.
V3.1-01  2014-09-03:  Corrections to Nrdy_ resets. Modified logic for
Err_Fdbk. Using P_Alarm, P_Mode V3.1-01.
V3.1-02  2014-09-30:  Unchanged, but using P_Alarm V3.1-02.
============================================================
This Instruction controls a Pulse Output Valve with
analog position feedback and provides:

*  Program and Operator entry of target position in Percent Open
*  Ramping of position to target at specified Rate of Change (%/sec)
*  Scaling of Position Feedback input from Raw (I/O card) Units to percent
*  Monitoring of Interlock conditions which cause output to shed
*  Configurable shed to HOLD or to configured position
*  Monitoring of I/O communication faults
*  Alarms for Interlock Trip, Actuator Fault, I/O Fault
*  Opened, Closed status based on limit switches (if provided),
or based on Position Feedback and a configured threshold.
*  "Available" status for use by automation logic to know whether valve
      can be controlled by other objects.

Modes are provided by a P_Mode AOI instance.
Alarms are provided by P_Alarm AOI instances.
Modes of operation: Operator, Program, Override, Maintenance, Hand
============================================================
SHOW LOCAL STRINGS
============================================================
The STRINGs containing the text associated with each instance of this
instruction cannot be Input or Output Parameters, because STRINGs are not
 'atomic' types. (Inputs and Outputs must be SINT, INT, DINT, REAL, or BOOL.)

In order to make it easier to find and configure these STRINGs, this rung
was added.  THE JMP (and the LBL on the following rung)
MUST NOT BE REMOVED!!!

To view the STRINGs, go to the instruction instance (in LD or FBD), RIGHT-click
to bring up the context menu and select "Open Instruction Logic".
This rung will appear with the STRING values for the selected instance.
You may double-click the STRING values here to modify them as well.

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-15: Added Cfg_CVNavTag to displayed strings  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JMP(SkipText)CONCAT(Cfg_Desc,Cfg_Label,Cfg_Tag)LOWER(Cfg_EU,Cfg_CVNavTag)LOWER(Inf_Lib,Inf_Type);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
MODE PROCESSING
============================================================
This rung handles received Mode Inputs and Commands:

NOTE:  All Commands for Mode, Alarm are aliased directly to the
corresponding Commands in the contained P_Mode and P_Alarm AOIs.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(SkipText)P_Mode(Mode);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[This rung handles simple First-Come First-Served Ownership Arbitration.
If a requestor supplies a non-zero Owner ID and the current owner is NONE (zero),
Ownership is assigned to the requesting ID.  When that requestor sets the Owner ID back
to zero, Ownership is relinquished back to NONE.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[EQU(PSet_Owner,0) ,EQU(Val_Owner,0) ]MOV(PSet_Owner,Val_Owner);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
CONFIGURATION
============================================================
These rungs get Configuration data (such as timer presets) and distribute as needed.
Note that some Configuration data are used directly in-place.

This rung publishes the Values for the scaled range.
If this instruction is reverse scaled by reversing the scaled (EU) min and max configurations,
the values are swapped so the the EUMax Value is always greater than the EUMin Value.
This makes the HMI bargraph and trend coding easier, and provides values
that can be pinned to the CV EU configuration of a PID, so the AOut and PID ranges are in lockstep.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[GEQ(Cfg_CVEUMax,Cfg_CVEUMin) MOV(Cfg_CVEUMin,Val_CVEUMin) MOV(Cfg_CVEUMax,Val_CVEUMax) ,LES(Cfg_CVEUMax,Cfg_CVEUMin) MOV(Cfg_CVEUMin,Val_CVEUMax) MOV(Cfg_CVEUMax,Val_CVEUMin) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Enter the maximum allowed Rate of Change Setting (in EU/sec)

If the rate is negative, infinite or not a number, no valid Rate of Change can be entered.  Set the maximum to zero.
This will force the CV Rate of Change to zero, which does NO ramping/limiting.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[LES(Cfg_MaxCVRoCInc,0.0) ,GRT(Cfg_MaxCVRoCInc,3.40282347E38) ]MOV(0.0,Cfg_MaxCVRoCInc);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Enter the maximum allowed Rate of Change Setting (in EU/sec)

If the rate is negative, infinite or not a number, no valid Rate of Change can be entered.  Set the maximum to zero.
This will force the CV Rate of Change to zero, which does NO ramping/limiting.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[LES(Cfg_MaxCVRoCDec,0.0) ,GRT(Cfg_MaxCVRoCDec,3.40282347E38) ]MOV(0.0,Cfg_MaxCVRoCDec);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the Valve does not have an Open Limit Switch input,
it cannot be used for Valve feedback checking.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Cfg_HasOpenLS)OTU(Cfg_UseOpenLS);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="7" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the Valve does not have a Closed Limit Switch input,
it cannot be used for Valve feedback checking.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Cfg_HasClosedLS)OTU(Cfg_UseClosedLS);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="8" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Flag a Configuration Error if the CV scaled range, or the CV output or
position feedback input range maximum and minimum are set the same (invalid range).]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[EQU(Cfg_CVEUMax,Cfg_CVEUMin) OTE(Err_EU) ,EQU(Cfg_FdbkRawMax,Cfg_FdbkRawMin) OTE(Err_Raw) ,EQU(Cfg_CVRawMax,Cfg_CVRawMin) OTE(Err_Fdbk) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="9" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Check for a valid Cycle Time.  If it results in a negative value, clear it and flag it.

The other times must be set relative to the Cycle Time.  If any is invalid, just make it valid:
* If Max ON Time < 0 or > Cycle Time, set it to Cycle Time.
*  If Min ON Time < 0 or > Max ON Time, set it to zero.
* If Deadtime < 0 or > Cycle Time, set it to zero.
* Bump Time must be within the Min ON Time and Max ON Time.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[MUL(Cfg_CycleT,1000,Wrk_CycleT_ms) LES(Wrk_CycleT_ms,0) CLR(Wrk_CycleT_ms) OTE(Err_Timer) ,[LES(Cfg_MaxOnT,0.0) ,GRT(Cfg_MaxOnT,Cfg_CycleT) ] MOV(Cfg_CycleT,Cfg_MaxOnT) ,[LES(Cfg_MinOnT,0.0) ,GRT(Cfg_MinOnT,Cfg_CycleT) ] CLR(Cfg_MinOnT) ,[LES(Cfg_Deadtime,0.0) ,GRT(Cfg_Deadtime,Cfg_MaxOnT) ] CLR(Cfg_Deadtime) ,LES(Cfg_BumpT,Cfg_MinOnT) NEQ(Cfg_BumpT,0.0) MOV(Cfg_MinOnT,Cfg_BumpT) ,GRT(Cfg_BumpT,Cfg_MaxOnT) MOV(Cfg_MaxOnT,Cfg_BumpT) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="10" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Clamp other configuration parameters to ensure they are valid.

* If the Open Rate < 0, set it to zero.
* If the Close Rate < 0, set it to zero.
* Clamp the Closed/Opened status threshold to be within the
scaled range CVEUMin to CVEUMax.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[LES(Cfg_OpenRate,0.0) CLR(Cfg_OpenRate) ,LES(Cfg_CloseRate,0.0) CLR(Cfg_CloseRate) ,LES(Cfg_MaxClosedPos,Cfg_CVEUMin) MOV(Cfg_CVEUMin,Cfg_MaxClosedPos) ,GRT(Cfg_MaxClosedPos,Cfg_CVEUMax) MOV(Cfg_CVEUMax,Cfg_MaxClosedPos) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="11" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[This rung handles the reports of all Bad Configuration Status.
Individual bits are provided for various status:
Crossed CV Limits or Invalid Rate of Change Limit
Bad Cycle Time configuration
Bad Raw Scaling configs (Max = Min)
Bad Alarm configs

Then a summary Bad Config status is provided, simply an OR of the individual bits.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[LES(Cfg_MaxCV,Cfg_MinCV) OTE(Err_Limit) ,XIC(Err_EU) ,XIC(Err_Raw) ,XIC(Err_Fdbk) ,XIC(Err_Timer) ,[XIC(IntlkTrip.Sts_Err) ,XIC(ActuatorFault.Sts_Err) ,XIC(IOFault.Sts_Err) ] OTE(Err_Alarm) ]OTE(Sts_Err);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="12" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
FEEDBACK PROCESSING
============================================================
Always show the actual position from the Position Feedback input.
If the input is a valid REAL number, convert it from raw units to percent open.
If the input is Infinite or Not A Number, just copy that bit pattern across.

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22:  Added check for Err_Fdbk on scaling of position feedback.  #####
#####  V3.1-01  2014-09-03:  Modified check for Err_Fdbk.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Inp_Sim)[COP(Inp_PosFdbk,Wrk_RealChkDINT,1) MEQ(Wrk_RealChkDINT,16#7F80_0000,16#7F80_0000) OTE(Sts_PosInfNaN) ,[XIC(Sts_PosInfNaN) ,XIC(Err_Fdbk) ] COP(Inp_PosFdbk,Val_Pos,1) ,XIO(Sts_PosInfNaN) XIO(Err_Fdbk) CPT(Val_Pos,(Inp_PosFdbk-Cfg_FdbkRawMin)*(Cfg_CVEUMax-Cfg_CVEUMin)/(Cfg_FdbkRawMax-Cfg_FdbkRawMin)+Cfg_CVEUMin) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="13" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[In preparation for rate limiting and ramping, this rung
determines the time since the previous scan, using a non-stop TON timer.
When we get here, we read the accumulator (in milliseconds, so
divide by 1000.0 to get seconds), then clear the accumulator.  Next scan the accumulator will have
incremented up to the number of milliseconds since last scan.  (Works for scan times up to 2 million seconds.....)]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(2147483647,Wrk_ScanT.PRE)TON(Wrk_ScanT,?,?)DIV(Wrk_ScanT.ACC,1000.0,Wrk_ScanTime)CLR(Wrk_ScanT.ACC);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="14" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[In simulation, the simulated position simply ramps as the configured
valve Open Rate or Close Rate when the logic is requesting the valve
to open or close.  (Note Wrk_ bits, not Out_ bits, which are cleared in sim.)]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Inp_Sim)[XIC(Wrk_Open) CPT(Val_Pos,Val_Pos+(Wrk_ScanTime*Cfg_OpenRate)) ,XIC(Wrk_Close) CPT(Val_Pos,Val_Pos-(Wrk_ScanTime*Cfg_OpenRate)) ,GRT(Val_Pos,Cfg_MaxCV) MOV(Cfg_MaxCV,Val_Pos) ,LES(Val_Pos,Cfg_MinCV) MOV(Cfg_MinCV,Val_Pos) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="15" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
SETTINGS PROCESSING
============================================================
This rung handles received Settings:

Limit the Program and Operator CV Settings to the hard limits, regardless of Mode
(in case tracking is off....).
When the Mode is Program, use the Program Settings for the CV and Rate of Change Limit..
When the Mode is Operator or Maintenance, use the Operator Settings.
When the Mode is Override, use the Override CV input for the CV.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[[[GRT(PSet_CV,Cfg_MaxCV) MOV(Cfg_MaxCV,PSet_CV) ,LES(PSet_CV,Cfg_MinCV) MOV(Cfg_MinCV,PSet_CV) ] XIC(Sts_Prog) ,XIO(Sts_Maint) XIO(Sts_Hand) [GRT(OSet_CV,Cfg_MaxCV) MOV(Cfg_MaxCV,OSet_CV) ,LES(OSet_CV,Cfg_MinCV) MOV(Cfg_MinCV,OSet_CV) ] ] OTE(Sts_Clamped) ,XIC(Sts_Prog) MOV(PSet_CV,Wrk_CV) ,[XIC(Sts_Oper) ,XIC(Sts_Maint) ] MOV(OSet_CV,Wrk_CV) ,XIC(Sts_Ovrd) MOV(Inp_OvrdCV,Wrk_CV) ,XIC(Mode.Sts_ProgOperSel) MOV(PSet_CVRoCLimInc,Wrk_CVRoCLimInc) MOV(PSet_CVRoCLimDec,Wrk_CVRoCLimDec) ,XIO(Mode.Sts_ProgOperSel) MOV(OSet_CVRoCLimInc,Wrk_CVRoCLimInc) MOV(OSet_CVRoCLimDec,Wrk_CVRoCLimDec) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="16" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Always limit the entered Rate of Change Limit to be between 0.0 and the configured Maximum.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[[GRT(Wrk_CVRoCLimInc,Cfg_MaxCVRoCInc) ,LES(Wrk_CVRoCLimInc,0.0) ,EQU(Wrk_CVRoCLimInc,0.0) XIO(Sts_Maint) ] MOV(Cfg_MaxCVRoCInc,Wrk_CVRoCLimInc) ,[GRT(Wrk_CVRoCLimDec,Cfg_MaxCVRoCDec) ,LES(Wrk_CVRoCLimDec,0.0) ,EQU(Wrk_CVRoCLimDec,0.0) XIO(Sts_Maint) ] MOV(Cfg_MaxCVRoCDec,Wrk_CVRoCLimDec) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="17" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
INTERLOCK / PERMISSIVE BYPASS / CHECK COMMAND HANDLING
============================================================
The Bypass Command bypasses the Bypassable Permissives and Interlocks.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(OCmd_Bypass)OTU(OCmd_Bypass)OTL(Wrk_Bypass);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="18" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[The Check Command enables checking of ALL
Permissives and Interlocks (cancels the bypass).]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(OCmd_Check)OTU(OCmd_Check)OTU(Wrk_Bypass);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="19" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the internal Bypass flag is set, Bypassable Interlocks ARE BYPASSED.
Bypassing is ACTIVE if Bypass selected OR in Maintenance Mode,
or in Override Mode if Override Bypassing is configured.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(Wrk_Bypass) OTE(Sts_Bypass) ,XIC(Sts_Maint) ,XIC(Sts_Ovrd) XIC(Cfg_OvrdIntlk) ]OTE(Sts_BypActive);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="20" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If a Maintenance Bypass function is active, display the Maintenance Bypass
Indicator (triangle symbol) on the graphic symbol.  Bypass functions are:
*  Interlock Bypass function is enabled.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Sts_Bypass)OTE(Sts_MaintByp);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="21" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Ready for Interlock Bypass if not bypassed.
Ready to cancel Bypass (resume checking) if bypassed.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIO(Wrk_Bypass) OTE(Rdy_Bypass) ,XIC(Wrk_Bypass) OTE(Rdy_Check) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="22" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
DEVICE DISABLE/ENABLE COMMAND HANDLING
============================================================
The Disable Command disables the Analog Output.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(MCmd_Disable)OTU(MCmd_Disable)XIC(Mode.Rdy_MRel)OTL(Wrk_Disabled);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="23" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[The Enable Command cancels the Disable and enables the Analog Output.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(MCmd_Enable)OTU(MCmd_Enable)XIC(Mode.Rdy_MRel)OTU(Wrk_Disabled);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="24" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the internal Disabled flag is set, the Drive is DISABLED.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Wrk_Disabled)OTE(Sts_Disabled);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="25" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Ready to Disable device if in Maintenance Mode and device is NOT Disabled.
Ready to Enable device if in Maintenance Mode and device IS Disabled.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Mode.Rdy_MRel)[XIO(Wrk_Disabled) OTE(Rdy_Disable) ,XIC(Wrk_Disabled) OTE(Rdy_Enable) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="26" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
ALARM RESET COMMAND HANDLING
============================================================
This rung handles the Alarm Reset Input and Commands
and the "Reset and Ack All" Operator Command.

This rung processes received Reset Commands from:
1.  The Operator via HMI Operator Command, or
2.  Higher-level strategies via Program Command
3.  Pushbuttons or other block via Input
and forwards the Reset to ALL Alarms and latched Shed conditions.

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22: Added Not Ready reason bits (Nrdy_xxx)  #####
#####  V3.1-01  2014-09-03:  Corrections to Nrdy_ trip and reset logic.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(OCmd_Reset) OTU(OCmd_Reset) ,XIC(OCmd_ResetAckAll) OTU(OCmd_ResetAckAll) [OTL(IntlkTrip.PCmd_Ack) ,OTL(ActuatorFault.PCmd_Ack) ,OTL(IOFault.PCmd_Ack) ] ,XIC(PCmd_Reset) [XIC(Cfg_PCmdClear) OTU(PCmd_Reset) ,NOP() ] ,XIC(Inp_Reset) ][OTL(IntlkTrip.PCmd_Reset) OTL(ActuatorFault.PCmd_Reset) OTL(IOFault.PCmd_Reset) ,[XIO(Inp_IOFault) ,XIC(Inp_Sim) ] OTU(Nrdy_IOFault) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="27" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[The Pulse Output Valve is NOT READY to be controlled (by the owner specified by Mode) if it is
Disabled, if interlocks are not OK (accounting for bypassing), I/O Communication is faulted
or there is a configuration error.

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22: Added Not Ready reason bits (Nrdy_xxx)  #####
#####  V3.1-01  2014-09-03:  Corrections to Nrdy_ trip and reset logic.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[OTU(Nrdy_NoMode)[XIC(Sts_Disabled) OTE(Nrdy_Disabled) ,XIC(Sts_Err) OTE(Nrdy_CfgErr) ,[XIO(Inp_IntlkOK) XIO(Sts_BypActive) ,XIO(Inp_NBIntlkOK) ] [ONS(Wrk_IntlkTripONS) [XIO(Cfg_ShedHold) NEQ(Wrk_CV,Wrk_CVOut) ,XIC(Cfg_ShedHold) [NEQ(Wrk_OpenTime,0.0) ,NEQ(Wrk_CloseTime,0.0) ] ] OTE(Sts_IntlkTrip) ,OTE(Nrdy_Intlk) ] ,XIC(Inp_IOFault) XIO(Inp_Sim) OTE(Sts_IOFault) XIC(Cfg_ShedOnIOFault) OTL(Nrdy_IOFault) ,XIC(Nrdy_IOFault) ]OTE(Sts_NotRdy);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="28" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[The device is AVAILABLE to be controlled by other objects
if it IS in Program Mode and it IS NOT "Not Ready".]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Sts_Prog)XIO(Sts_NotRdy)OTE(Sts_Available);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="29" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Ready to receive a new OSet_CV if tracking is NOT enabled,
or we're in Operator or Maintenance Mode, or,
if we're in Hand or Ovrd, tracking is NOT configured for Ovrd and Hand.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIO(Cfg_SetTrack) ,XIC(Sts_Oper) ,XIC(Sts_Maint) ,[XIC(Sts_Hand) ,XIC(Sts_Ovrd) ] XIO(Cfg_SetTrackOvrdHand) ]OTE(Rdy_CV);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="30" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Ready to receive other Operator Settings (OSet_xxx) if tracking is NOT enabled,
or we're in Operator or Maintenance Mode.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIO(Cfg_SetTrack) ,XIC(Sts_Oper) ,XIC(Sts_Maint) ][GRT(Cfg_MaxCVRoCInc,0.0) OTE(Rdy_CVRoCLimInc) ,GRT(Cfg_MaxCVRoCDec,0.0) OTE(Rdy_CVRoCLimDec) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="31" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
DEVICE FEEDBACK PROCESSING
============================================================
If the Valve is NOT being simulated and it HAS and IS USING limit switch feedback,
use the state of the limit switch feedback inputs as the Valve position.

If the Valve is being simulated or has no limit switch feedback, use the Sim Timers to provide
"fake" feedback signals based on the simulation time to Open and time to Close.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIO(Inp_Sim) [XIC(Cfg_UseOpenLS) XIC(Inp_OpenLS) ,XIO(Cfg_UseOpenLS) XIC(Cfg_UseClosedLS) XIO(Inp_ClosedLS) ] ,[XIC(Inp_Sim) ,XIO(Cfg_UseOpenLS) XIO(Cfg_UseClosedLS) ] XIO(Sts_PosInfNaN) GRT(Val_Pos,Cfg_MaxClosedPos) ]OTE(Wrk_OpenFdbk);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="32" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the Valve is NOT being simulated and it HAS and IS USING limit switch feedback,
use the state of the limit switch feedback inputs as the Valve position.

If the Valve is being simulated or has no limit switch feedback, use the Sim Timers to provide
"fake" feedback signals based on the simulation time to Open and time to Close.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIO(Inp_Sim) [XIC(Cfg_UseClosedLS) XIC(Inp_ClosedLS) ,XIO(Cfg_UseClosedLS) XIC(Cfg_UseOpenLS) XIO(Inp_OpenLS) ] ,[XIC(Inp_Sim) ,XIO(Cfg_UseClosedLS) XIO(Cfg_UseOpenLS) ] XIO(Sts_PosInfNaN) LEQ(Val_Pos,Cfg_MaxClosedPos) ]OTE(Wrk_ClosedFdbk);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="33" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
VALVE FEEDBACK STATUS
============================================================
This rung generates the Valve Feedback Status bits and Enumeration Value:
Limit Switch Fail, if the Limit Switches are in the fail position (won't be seen in Simulation)
Opened, if the valve is confirmed opened
Closed, if the valve is confirmed closed
Moving, if the Limit Switches show travel

If the valve is being reported Open, set the "Active" status (in common with P_PID and
P_AOut) to show the analog output graphic symbol on the HMI as "active" (e.g., valve open).]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[[XIC(Cfg_LSFail) XIC(Wrk_OpenFdbk) XIC(Wrk_ClosedFdbk) ,XIO(Cfg_LSFail) XIO(Wrk_OpenFdbk) XIO(Wrk_ClosedFdbk) ] OTE(Sts_LSFail) MOV(3,Val_Fdbk) ,XIC(Wrk_OpenFdbk) XIO(Wrk_ClosedFdbk) MOV(2,Val_Fdbk) ,XIC(Wrk_ClosedFdbk) XIO(Wrk_OpenFdbk) MOV(1,Val_Fdbk) ,[XIC(Cfg_LSFail) XIO(Wrk_OpenFdbk) XIO(Wrk_ClosedFdbk) ,XIO(Cfg_LSFail) XIC(Wrk_OpenFdbk) XIC(Wrk_ClosedFdbk) ] CLR(Val_Fdbk) ,NEQ(Val_Fdbk,1) OTE(Sts_Active) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="34" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
BUMP OPEN, BUMP CLOSE COMMAND HANDLING
============================================================
Ready to receive Bump Open / Bump Close Commands when:
*  Bump Timer preset is greater than zero (zero disables the bump function)
*  The Position Feedback is Infinite, Not a Number, or
-  there is an I/O fault, or
-  there is an actuator fault
* The valve is in Operator or Maintenance Mode
* A previous Bump operation is not active.
*  The valve is not at the end of travel (or limit switch is not being used)
for the target direction of travel.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(Sts_PosInfNaN) ,XIC(Sts_IOFault) ,XIC(Sts_ActuatorFault) ][XIC(Sts_Oper) ,XIC(Sts_Maint) ]XIO(Wrk_BumpOpen)XIO(Wrk_BumpClose)XIO(Wrk_BumpingOpen)XIO(Wrk_BumpingClose)[[XIO(Wrk_OpenFdbk) ,XIO(Cfg_UseClosedLS) ,XIO(Cfg_UseOpenLS) ] OTE(Rdy_BumpOpen) ,[XIO(Wrk_ClosedFdbk) ,XIO(Cfg_UseClosedLS) ,XIO(Cfg_UseOpenLS) ] OTE(Rdy_BumpClose) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="35" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[The Bump Open Command is used to pulse the Open Output
when the valve position feedback is not available (Inf, NaN, I/O Fault).]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(OCmd_BumpOpen)OTU(OCmd_BumpOpen)XIC(Rdy_BumpOpen)OTL(Wrk_BumpOpen);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="36" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[The Bump Close Command is used to pulse the Close Output
when the valve position feedback is not available (Inf, NaN, I/O Fault).]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(OCmd_BumpClose)OTU(OCmd_BumpClose)XIC(Rdy_BumpClose)OTL(Wrk_BumpClose);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="37" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
VALVE ACTUATOR FAULT DETECTION
============================================================
If the Actuator Fault Input is true and we're not in simulation.
raise an ACTUATOR FAULT Status.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Inp_ActuatorFault)XIO(Inp_Sim)OTE(Sts_ActuatorFault);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="38" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Clear flags for capturing exception conditions.

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22:  Added SrcQ, SrcQ_IO enumerations.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[OTU(Wrk_ShedHold)OTU(Wrk_ShedIntlkCV);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="39" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If Interlocks are not OK (non-bypassable always checked, bypassable checked if not bypassed
(bypassables not checked in Maintenance Mode, or in Override Mode if Override ignores bypassable interlocks...),
or if there is an I/O Fault, either HOLD the valve position or SHED it to the configured Interlock CV,
depending on the state of the Shed/Hold configuration.

We don't want the Interlock Trip alarm occuring if Hand or Disabled is going to win down below....

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22: Added Not Ready reason bits (Nrdy_xxx)  #####
#####  V3.1-01  2014-09-03:  Corrections to Nrdy_ trip and reset logic.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(Nrdy_Intlk) ,XIC(Nrdy_IOFault) ]XIO(Sts_Hand)XIO(Wrk_Disabled)XIO(Sts_Err)[XIO(Cfg_ShedHold) OTL(Wrk_ShedIntlkCV) MOV(Cfg_IntlkCV,Wrk_CV) ,XIC(Cfg_ShedHold) XIO(Sts_PosInfNaN) OTL(Wrk_ShedHold) MOV(Val_Pos,Wrk_CV) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="40" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the Pulse Output Valve is DISABLED or in Hand, or the Configuration is not valid,
set the CV to the feedback position (and do not energize pulse outputs -- handled elsewhere).]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(Sts_Hand) ,XIC(Sts_Disabled) ,XIC(Sts_Err) ]XIO(Sts_PosInfNaN)MOV(Val_Pos,Wrk_CV);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="41" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[After all that selecting, copy the final result to a holding register.  (We needed it above to do
the HOLD function on Interlock!)

This is where the CV will be when ramping (if any) is done.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(Wrk_CV,Wrk_CVSet);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="42" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[This rung determines whether the selected Position CV is +/- Infinity or +/- NotANumber.
In either case, the value cannot be passed into the rate limiter calculation or to the final output.
Instead, perform the designated SHED action: hold last output or shed to the Interlock CV.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[COP(Wrk_CVSet,Wrk_RealChkDINT,1)MEQ(Wrk_RealChkDINT,16#7F80_0000,16#7F80_0000)[XIO(Cfg_ShedHold) OTL(Wrk_ShedIntlkCV) MOV(Cfg_IntlkCV,Wrk_CVSet) ,XIC(Cfg_ShedHold) OTL(Wrk_ShedHold) MOV(Wrk_CVOut,Wrk_CVSet) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="43" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Under these conditions, SKIP the output ramping:

* The AOut is disabled
* The AOut is in Hand
* Rate of Change Skip is configures TRUE and, either, the Mode is Maintenance,
the Mode is Override, or an Interlock holds us out.
* The entered CV Rate of Change Limit is ZERO.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(Cfg_SkipRoCLim) [XIC(Sts_Maint) ,XIC(Sts_Ovrd) ,XIO(Inp_NBIntlkOK) ,XIO(Inp_IntlkOK) XIO(Sts_BypActive) ] ,EQU(Wrk_CVRoCLimInc,0.0) EQU(Wrk_CVRoCLimDec,0.0) ,XIC(Sts_Hand) ,XIC(Sts_Disabled) ,XIC(Sts_Err) ]OTE(Sts_SkipRoCLim)MOV(Wrk_CVSet,Wrk_CVOut);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="44" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the conditions above are NOT TRUE, do the rate limiting:

If the time since the previous scan is not ZERO:

* Calculate the amount to step this scan toward the final CV
* If we're within a single setp, go to the final CV
* If we're not within a single step, step one step closer (up or down, whichever way we need to go)
and show the "Ramping" status.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Sts_SkipRoCLim)NEQ(Wrk_CVSet,Wrk_CVOut)NEQ(Wrk_ScanTime,0)[MUL(Wrk_CVRoCLimInc,Wrk_ScanTime,Wrk_StepInc) MUL(Wrk_CVRoCLimDec,Wrk_ScanTime,Wrk_StepDec) ,CMP(Wrk_CVOut<=(Wrk_CVSet+Wrk_StepDec)) CMP(Wrk_CVOut>=(Wrk_CVSet-Wrk_StepInc)) MOV(Wrk_CVSet,Wrk_CVOut) ,[CMP(Wrk_CVOut>(Wrk_CVSet+Wrk_StepDec)) SUB(Wrk_CVOut,Wrk_StepDec,Wrk_CVOut) ,CMP(Wrk_CVOut<=(Wrk_CVSet-Wrk_StepInc)) ADD(Wrk_CVOut,Wrk_StepInc,Wrk_CVOut) ] OTE(Sts_Ramping) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="45" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
OUTPUT GENERATION
============================================================
The following rungs generate the instruction's outputs, 
on this rung the CV analog output (Out_CV)

If not in simulation, scale the generated Wrk_CVOut to Raw Units in Out_CV
If in Simulation, set Out_CV to zero.

Ffixed scaling CPT -- previously, raw and EU were swapped. Fixed Err_EU and Err_Raw.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIO(Inp_Sim) [XIO(Err_EU) XIO(Err_Raw) CPT(Out_CV,(Wrk_CVOut-Cfg_CVEUMin)*(Cfg_CVRawMax-Cfg_CVRawMin)/(Cfg_CVEUMax-Cfg_CVEUMin)+Cfg_CVRawMin) ,[XIC(Err_EU) ,XIC(Err_Raw) ] OTL(Wrk_ShedIntlkCV) MOV(Cfg_IntlkCV,Out_CV) ] ,XIC(Inp_Sim) CLR(Out_CV) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="46" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[The following rungs generate the instruction's Open and Close pulse outputs.

If the Cycle Time is > 0, and the position and CV are valid real numbers, then:

* the open output can be energized if the valve CV > Position, we're not
up against the open limit switch, the position is less than the Max CV,
and the valve has an Open Rate > 0.

* the close output can be energized if the valve Position > CV, we're not
up against the closed limit switch, the position is more than the Min CV,
and the valve has a Close Rate > 0.

]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[GRT(Cfg_CycleT,0.0)XIO(Sts_PosInfNaN)XIO(Sts_CVInfNaN)[GRT(Val_CVOut,Val_Pos) [XIO(Inp_OpenLS) ,XIO(Cfg_UseOpenLS) ,XIC(Inp_Sim) ] LES(Val_Pos,Cfg_MaxCV) GRT(Cfg_OpenRate,0.0) OTE(Wrk_OKtoOpen) ,LES(Val_CVOut,Val_Pos) [XIO(Inp_ClosedLS) ,XIO(Cfg_UseClosedLS) ,XIC(Inp_Sim) ] GRT(Val_Pos,Cfg_MinCV) GRT(Cfg_CloseRate,0.0) OTE(Wrk_OKtoClose) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="47" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[This is just a continuously-running Cycle Timer.  By not using the Cycle
Timer ".DN" (done) bit on the rung, we avoid losing the one scan of
time when the timer is not running in an XIO / TON rung.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[TON(Wrk_CycleT,?,?);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="48" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[When the Cycle completes, calculate a new Open Time (time to pulse the
Open output) and Close Time (time to pulse the Close output).

Details of this calculation are in the Instruction Help for the POSP built-in
instruction, from which this logic is derived.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[GEQ(Wrk_CycleT.ACC,Wrk_CycleT_ms) ,XIO(Wrk_CycleActive) ][OTU(Wrk_CycleActive) OTU(Wrk_BumpingOpen) OTU(Wrk_BumpingClose) CLR(Wrk_CycleT.ACC) ,XIC(Wrk_OKtoOpen) [CPT(Wrk_OpenTime,(Val_CVOut-Val_Pos)/Cfg_OpenRate) ,LES(Wrk_OpenTimePrev,Cfg_CycleT) ADD(Wrk_OpenTime,Cfg_Deadtime,Wrk_OpenTime) ,GRT(Wrk_OpenTime,Cfg_MaxOnT) MOV(Cfg_MaxOnT,Wrk_OpenTime) ,LES(Wrk_OpenTime,Cfg_MinOnT) CLR(Wrk_OpenTime) ] ,XIO(Wrk_OKtoOpen) XIO(Wrk_BumpOpen) CLR(Wrk_OpenTime) ,XIC(Wrk_BumpOpen) OTU(Wrk_BumpOpen) OTL(Wrk_BumpingOpen) MOV(Cfg_BumpT,Wrk_OpenTime) ,XIC(Wrk_OKtoClose) [CPT(Wrk_CloseTime,(Val_Pos-Val_CVOut)/Cfg_CloseRate) ,LES(Wrk_CloseTimePrev,Cfg_CycleT) ADD(Wrk_CloseTime,Cfg_Deadtime,Wrk_CloseTime) ,GRT(Wrk_CloseTime,Cfg_MaxOnT) MOV(Cfg_MaxOnT,Wrk_CloseTime) ,LES(Wrk_CloseTime,Cfg_MinOnT) CLR(Wrk_CloseTime) ] ,XIO(Wrk_OKtoClose) XIO(Wrk_BumpClose) CLR(Wrk_CloseTime) ,XIC(Wrk_BumpClose) OTU(Wrk_BumpClose) OTL(Wrk_BumpingClose) MOV(Cfg_BumpT,Wrk_CloseTime) ,[NEQ(Wrk_OpenTime,0.0) ,NEQ(Wrk_CloseTime,0.0) ] OTL(Wrk_CycleActive) ,MOV(Wrk_OpenTime,Wrk_OpenTimePrev) ,MOV(Wrk_CloseTime,Wrk_CloseTimePrev) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="49" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the Open output should be energized and the timer accumulator hasn't reached the Open time yet
(could be less than the cycle time, even zero), energize the Open request.
(We use a Work bit so we can send this to the position simulation logic above.)]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(Wrk_OKtoOpen) XIO(Inp_ActuatorFault) ,XIC(Wrk_BumpingOpen) ]CMP(Wrk_CycleT.ACC<(Wrk_OpenTime*1000.0))OTE(Wrk_Open);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="50" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the Close output should be energized and the timer accumulator hasn't reached the Close time yet
(could be less than the cycle time, even zero), energize the Close request.
(We use a Work bit so we can send this to the position simulation logic above.)]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(Wrk_OKtoClose) XIO(Inp_ActuatorFault) ,XIC(Wrk_BumpingClose) ]CMP(Wrk_CycleT.ACC<(Wrk_CloseTime*1000.0))OTE(Wrk_Close);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="51" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If were not in simulation and the actuator is not faulted, go ahead and energize the real outputs
as determined by the logic above.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Inp_Sim)[XIC(Wrk_Open) OTE(Out_Open) ,XIC(Wrk_Close) OTE(Out_Close) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="52" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
VALUES PROCESSING
============================================================

Copy the working CV values to their "Val_" output parameters, and based on Mode
and the Tracking configs, also copy them back into the Operator and Program Settings.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[MOV(Wrk_CVSet,Val_CVSet) MOV(Wrk_CVOut,Val_CVOut) MOV(Wrk_CVRoCLimInc,Val_CVRoCLimInc) MOV(Wrk_CVRoCLimDec,Val_CVRoCLimDec) ,XIC(Cfg_SetTrack) [[XIO(Sts_Ovrd) XIO(Sts_Hand) ,XIC(Cfg_SetTrackOvrdHand) ] MOV(Val_CVSet,PSet_CV) MOV(Val_CVSet,OSet_CV) ,MOV(Val_CVRoCLimInc,PSet_CVRoCLimInc) MOV(Val_CVRoCLimInc,OSet_CVRoCLimInc) MOV(Val_CVRoCLimDec,PSet_CVRoCLimDec) MOV(Val_CVRoCLimDec,OSet_CVRoCLimDec) ] ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="53" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Val_Sts is the current device status, as determined by this AOI,
based on its knowledge of settings and configuration.
(This is the status that should be monitored by, for example, FT Historian.)
0 = At Target
1 = Ramping Down
2 = Ramping Up
3 = Clamped at CVMin
4 = Clamped at CVMax
5 = Bump Close requested or currently pulsing Out_Close
6 = Bump Open requested or currently pulsing Out_Open
32 = I/O Fault
33 = Disabled
34 = Configuration Error

#####  V3.0-04  2014-01-20:  Corrected generation of Val_Sts, Val_Fault.  #####
#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22: Added Not Ready reason bits (Nrdy_xxx)  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[CLR(Wrk_Sts) CLR(Wrk_Fault) ,XIC(Sts_Ramping) [GRT(Wrk_CVOut,Wrk_CVSet) MOV(1,Wrk_Sts) ,LES(Wrk_CVOut,Wrk_CVSet) MOV(2,Wrk_Sts) ] ,XIC(Sts_Clamped) [EQU(Wrk_CVOut,Cfg_MinCV) MOV(3,Wrk_Sts) ,EQU(Wrk_CVOut,Cfg_MaxCV) MOV(4,Wrk_Sts) ] ,[XIC(Wrk_BumpClose) ,XIC(Wrk_BumpingClose) ] OTE(Sts_BumpClose) MOV(5,Wrk_Sts) ,[XIC(Wrk_BumpOpen) ,XIC(Wrk_BumpingOpen) ] OTE(Sts_BumpOpen) MOV(6,Wrk_Sts) ,XIC(Nrdy_IOFault) MOV(32,Wrk_Fault) ,XIC(Sts_Disabled) MOV(33,Wrk_Sts) ,XIC(Sts_Err) MOV(34,Wrk_Fault) ,MOV(Wrk_Sts,Val_Sts) MOV(Wrk_Fault,Val_Fault) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="54" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
ENUMERATED SOURCE / QUALITY VALUES
============================================================
Source and Quality for Input / Output values / status (SrcQ_IO)

0 = Good: live, confirmed good;   1 = Good: live, assumed good;
2 = Good: no feedback, assumed good;   8 = Test: Simulated;   9 = Test: Loopback;
10 = Test: Manually Entered;   16 = Uncertain: Live, off-spec;
17 = Uncertain, Substituted at Device / Bus;   18 = Uncertain, Substituted at AOI;
19 = Uncertain, using last known good;    20 = Uncertain, using replacement value;
32 = Bad, Signal Failure;   33 = Bad, Channel Fault;
34 = Bad, Module / Comms Fault;   35 = Bad, Invalid Configuration

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22:  Added rung for Source/Quality enumerations.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[CLR(Wrk_SrcQ) ,[XIO(Cfg_HasOpenLS) ,XIO(Cfg_HasClosedLS) ] MOV(1,Wrk_SrcQ) ,XIO(Cfg_HasOpenLS) XIO(Cfg_HasClosedLS) MOV(2,Wrk_SrcQ) ,XIC(Inp_ActuatorFault) MOV(32,Wrk_SrcQ) ,XIC(Inp_IOFault) MOV(34,Wrk_SrcQ) ,XIC(Inp_Sim) MOV(9,Wrk_SrcQ) ,MOV(Wrk_SrcQ,SrcQ_IO) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="55" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Source and Quality for primary values / status (SrcQ)

0 = Good: live, confirmed good;   1 = Good: live, assumed good;
2 = Good: no feedback, assumed good;   8 = Test: Simulated;   9 = Test: Loopback;
10 = Test: Manually Entered;   16 = Uncertain: Live, off-spec;
17 = Uncertain, Substituted at Device / Bus;   18 = Uncertain, Substituted at AOI;
19 = Uncertain, using last known good;    20 = Uncertain, using replacement value;
32 = Bad, Signal Failure;   33 = Bad, Channel Fault;
34 = Bad, Module / Comms Fault;   35 = Bad, Invalid Configuration

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22:  Added rung for Source/Quality enumerations.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[MOV(SrcQ_IO,Wrk_SrcQ) ,[XIC(Cfg_HasOpenLS) XIO(Cfg_UseOpenLS) ,XIC(Cfg_HasClosedLS) XIO(Cfg_UseClosedLS) ] MOV(18,Wrk_SrcQ) ,XIC(Wrk_ShedHold) MOV(19,Wrk_SrcQ) ,XIC(Wrk_ShedIntlkCV) MOV(20,Wrk_SrcQ) ,MOV(Wrk_SrcQ,SrcQ) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="56" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
ALARM PROCESSING
============================================================
Pass the Cfg_PCmdClear configuration down to each P_Alarm instance.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Cfg_PCmdClear)OTE(IntlkTrip.Cfg_PCmdClear)OTE(ActuatorFault.Cfg_PCmdClear)OTE(IOFault.Cfg_PCmdClear);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="57" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[The various alarms are ALWAYS allowed to Exist (Cfg_HasXxxAlarm can be 1).

#####  V3.0-00  2013-06-30:  Added PCfg_AllowExist on P_Alarm 3.0.  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[OTL(IntlkTrip.PCfg_AllowExist)OTL(ActuatorFault.PCfg_AllowExist)OTL(IOFault.PCfg_AllowExist);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="58" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Handle Interlock Trip Alarm Processing using the standard P_Alarm AOI.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[P_Alarm(IntlkTrip);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="59" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Handle Device-Reported Actuator Fault Alarm Processing using the standard P_Alarm AOI.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[P_Alarm(ActuatorFault);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="60" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Handle I/O Fault Alarm Processing using the standard P_Alarm AOI.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[P_Alarm(IOFault);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="61" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If ANY alarm is Disabled, Inhibited or Suppressed, set the Alarm Inhibit
summary status bit (which puts the "I" breadcrumb on the graphic symbol).]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(IntlkTrip.Sts_AlmInh) ,XIC(ActuatorFault.Sts_AlmInh) ,XIC(IOFault.Sts_AlmInh) ]OTE(Sts_AlmInh);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="62" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
NOTIFICATION VALUE
============================================================
Indicate the current alarm notification level as the max of any of the alarms,
and set the low-order bit for reset or ack required:
0 = Not in alarm, acknowledged
1 = Not in alarm, unacknowledged or reset required
2 = Low severity alarm, acknowledged
3 = Low severity alarm, unacknowledged
4 = Medium severity alarm, acknowledged
5 = Medium severity alarm, unacknowledged
6 = High severity alarm, acknowledged
7 = High severity alarm, unacknowledged
8 = Urgent severity alarm, acknowledged
9 = Urgent severity alarm, unacknowledged

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22:  Separated Reset Reqd functions from Val_Notify  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[CLR(Wrk_Notify) ,GRT(IntlkTrip.Val_Notify,Wrk_Notify) MOV(IntlkTrip.Val_Notify,Wrk_Notify) ,GRT(ActuatorFault.Val_Notify,Wrk_Notify) MOV(ActuatorFault.Val_Notify,Wrk_Notify) ,GRT(IOFault.Val_Notify,Wrk_Notify) MOV(IOFault.Val_Notify,Wrk_Notify) ,[XIC(IntlkTrip.Val_Notify.0) ,XIC(ActuatorFault.Val_Notify.0) ,XIC(IOFault.Val_Notify.0) ] OTE(Wrk_Notify.0) ,MOV(Wrk_Notify,Val_Notify) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="63" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[This rung identifies:
* if any alarm reset or other reset is required; and
* if any reset or acknowledge from the operator is required.

#####  V3.1-00  2014-07-22: Added Not Ready reason bits (Nrdy_xxx), split from Val_Notify logic (above).  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[[XIC(IntlkTrip.Rdy_Ack) ,XIC(ActuatorFault.Rdy_Ack) ,XIC(IOFault.Rdy_Ack) ] ,[XIC(IntlkTrip.Rdy_Reset) ,XIC(ActuatorFault.Rdy_Reset) ,XIC(IOFault.Rdy_Reset) ,XIC(Nrdy_IOFault) XIO(Sts_IOFault) ] OTE(Rdy_Reset) ]OTE(Rdy_ResetAckAll);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
<Routine Name="Prescan" Type="RLL">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Clear Ownership. clear "lurking" Commands]]>
</Description>
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
PRESCAN (POWERUP) HANDLING
============================================================
On Prescan, set the Ownership to NO OWNER (0).]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[CLR(PSet_Owner)CLR(Val_Owner);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA["Lurking" Operator Commands are cleared, as well as a Program Reset.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[OTU(OCmd_Bypass) OTU(OCmd_Check) OTU(MCmd_Disable) OTU(MCmd_Enable) ,OTU(OCmd_Reset) OTU(OCmd_ResetAckAll) OTU(PCmd_Reset) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
</Routines>
<Dependencies>
<Dependency Type="AddOnInstructionDefinition" Name="P_Alarm"/>
<Dependency Type="DataType" Name="STRING_20"/>
<Dependency Type="DataType" Name="STRING_40"/>
<Dependency Type="DataType" Name="STRING_8"/>
<Dependency Type="DataType" Name="STRING_12"/>
<Dependency Type="DataType" Name="STRING_16"/>
<Dependency Type="AddOnInstructionDefinition" Name="P_Mode"/>
</Dependencies>
</AddOnInstructionDefinition>
</AddOnInstructionDefinitions>
</Controller>
</RSLogix5000Content>
